const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Thought Leadership Content and Editorial Design","serviceType":"Content and editorial design","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/thought-leadership-content-editorial-design/","description":"Executive reports, white papers, bylined articles and editorial design for healthcare and life sciences — with the framework that actually governs each.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of content do you create?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Executive reports and white papers; bylined articles and opinion pieces under a named individual; blogs and short-form posts; investor, board and stakeholder material; conference and presentation versions of all of it; and the editorial design, layout, data visualisation and infographics that make any of it worth opening. Across healthcare, the life sciences, medical technology, health policy and the organisations that sell into or regulate them. We also do the unglamorous half: the argument page the programme hangs from, the claim log that makes every figure defensible, and the sourcing audit of content you already published."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did you remove COPE and ICMJE from your compliance section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because they do not govern this work. COPE and the ICMJE govern journal publication — authorship of research papers, editorial decisions, corrections, retractions, publication ethics. An executive report, an investor white paper, a blog or a ghost-written byline is none of those things and no journal body has any jurisdiction over them. Citing them on a corporate content page is not a higher standard; it is a compliance section copied from an academic page, and anybody who works in either field can see it. What actually governs a piece here is decided by three things: whether it names a medicine or device, in which case the promotional code of every market it reaches applies; whether money changed hands for the placement, in which case advertising-disclosure rules apply; and whether it is going to become a journal article, in which case — and only then — ICMJE and GPP 2022 do apply. The router above works it out. Note also that these frameworks were already correctly listed on our parent Scientific Communication page and were simply never inherited by this one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is ghostwriting acceptable?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Outside journals, writing with somebody is normal practice and no code prohibits it. Writing instead of somebody is a different thing, and the line is simple: could the named person defend every sentence in a room? Our working rule is that they are interviewed, they shape the argument, and they approve every claim before publication — and if a piece could go out under a name without that person having read it, it does not go out under that name. Inside journals the answer is different and much harder. Authorship is decided by the four ICMJE criteria, all of which every author must meet; a named individual who did not meet them cannot be an author, and a professional writer who is not acknowledged is the definition of the practice that publication ethics bodies were created to stop. Where the research is company-sponsored, GPP 2022 applies alongside. If a piece might travel from your website into a journal, decide that at the beginning, because retrofitting authorship is not possible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you guarantee reach or results?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and we will not make that claim. “Transforms rich insights into measurable growth” is offered across this market, almost always on a page containing no metric, case study or number of any kind. Growth has many inputs and a white paper is one of the smaller ones; anyone attributing pipeline to a single report is describing a correlation and calling it a cause. What this work reliably changes is narrower and more useful: your organisation has a defensible position written down, your people repeat it consistently, every figure in it survives being checked, and a hostile reader can find nothing in it that was not disclosed. If a supplier offers you a number for reach or leads, ask what the counterfactual was, and how they isolated it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Our piece mentions our product. What changes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most things. Material about a prescription medicine or a regulated device is capable of being promotion whatever format it takes — a white paper, a blog and a bylined article all count — and it is then governed by the promotional rules of every market it reaches. In the UK that is the ABPI Code, 2024 edition, administered by the PMCPA, over the Human Medicines Regulations 2012, under which advertising a prescription-only medicine to the general public is prohibited. In the EU it is the EFPIA Code over Directive 2001/83/EC, with the same prohibition. In the US it is FDA rules enforced by OPDP, plus the PhRMA and AdvaMed codes. In Canada, PAAB; in Australia, the Therapeutic Goods Advertising Code; in Japan, the PMD Act — which is what the Pharmaceutical Affairs Law was renamed in 2014, and is still widely cited under the old name. Our usual recommendation is to take the product out. Not to soften it — to remove it. A piece that argues at the level of the disease, the evidence or the health-system problem reaches a wider audience under far lighter rules, clears review faster, and is almost always the better piece. The sample on this page is exactly that decision."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is editorial design, and why does it cost more than layout?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Layout puts text into a template. Editorial design decides what the reader meets first, how the argument is visible before it is read, how long a reader can hold a line of type at that size, and which findings deserve a chart. The measurable difference is whether anyone finishes it. Most of the money in this category is spent on the part that looks like design — colour, illustration, cover — and most of the value is in the part that does not: hierarchy, structure and charts built from the data rather than drawn to a brief. In the sample on this page, two charts were removed for having nothing to say, one axis was rebuilt from zero because truncating it turned four points into an apparent doubling, and a six-colour palette became two plus grey so readers would stop consulting the legend. None of that photographs well and all of it is the work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you make it sound like us?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By interviewing the people whose voice it is supposed to be, which is the whole of the answer and is why the process starts with ninety minutes rather than a brief. A bylined piece built from a briefing document sounds like a briefing document. Beyond that: your style guide where you have one, your terminology, and two pieces you were genuinely happy with as reference. Where you have several style guides that disagree we will tell you, because that is usually the reason content from different teams does not read as one organisation. And where the named person’s own way of arguing is unusual, we keep it — a piece flattened into corporate house prose has lost the only thing that made it worth publishing under an individual’s name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can we publish the data in a white paper and then in a journal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes, and it is a decision to make at the start rather than discover afterwards. Journals differ in how they treat material already published in substance, and some will decline a paper whose central finding has appeared elsewhere. Check the prior-publication and preprint policy of any likely target journal before the white paper goes out. Two other things follow if a journal is in prospect: authorship has to be decided by the four ICMJE criteria rather than by who is senior, and any professional writing assistance is acknowledged rather than invisible. Where the research is company-sponsored, GPP 2022 applies. We will flag all of this at the argument-page stage, because the sequencing is free to get right and expensive to fix."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see a sample before commissioning?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and there is a complete one on this page — a build pack for an executive report that arrived as a product brief and was rebuilt as a health-system argument, with the sector, the pathway, the client and every figure changed. Open it above and read the whole thing, including the parts that were uncomfortable: three claims removed for having no traceable source, a chart axis that overstated a finding by an order of magnitude, and a recommendation to take the client’s own product out of their own white paper. If you want something closer to your own work, ask and we will build one around a piece like yours. Two caveats. Client work is confidential, so any sample is redacted or composite. And a sample shows method and sourcing discipline; it cannot show whether the writer found your argument, which is what the free ninety minutes is for."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Scientific communication","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/thought-leadership-content-editorial-design/"}]}];

